# SFT execution status v0.71

2026-09-10. **Real-sized import components are implemented and tiny CPU tests pass.**
This completes a bounded software-development stage, not the real 4B import or
Gate 5. No real weights, trainer payloads, tokenizer payloads or datasets were
read; no network operation, GPU, inference, training, instance action, dependency
installation, GitHub publication or CI run was initiated.

## Completed implementation

- `posttraining_checkpoint_import_plan_v1.py` integrates the corrected final-base
  metadata validator with the independently base-pinned handoff. It checks the
  handoff file/internal hashes, state/run/cursor/rank lineage and the complete
  checkpoint manifest. The whole 19-file inventory remains required, including
  trainer files. Tokenizer binding remains the original canonical JSON inventory,
  never a fabricated checksum-line file.
- `posttraining_checkpoint_stream_v1.py` verifies an exact closed tree using
  at most 1 MiB per content read. It uses pinned directory descriptors,
  no-symlink traversal, ownership/mode/single-link checks, before/after identity
  checks, an aggregate read bound and cooperative deadline. It reads each file
  once; the final walk checks metadata only. Late cleanup cannot publish success
  after the deadline. This is change detection for quiescent inputs, not an
  atomic snapshot against a hostile owner or a hard syscall timeout.
- `posttraining_checkpoint_import_torch_v3.py` is a new internal CPU conversion
  kernel with mandatory parameter, projected live-tensor and output limits plus
  a synchronous budget callback. It does not raise or reuse v2's synthetic caps.
  A meta-only architecture supplies exact roles/shapes. DCP destinations preserve
  both tied aliases independently until dtype/value equality is checked. Only
  parameters are cast; persistent rotary buffers preserve their values at FP32.
  Strict `assign=True` restoration, validated re-tying, chunked finite/equality/
  digest checks, staged tensor lifetimes and complete local HF reload comparison
  replace the large simultaneous-copy pattern in v2.

The source model configuration now admits only known scalar Lumi fields and the
original architecture metadata. Unknown quantization, remote-code, attention
loader or device-selection kwargs are rejected before ML dependencies. Generated
configuration serialization is bounded before output creation and its complete
saved content is compared before reload. The existing runtime has no Accelerate;
reload uses explicit `device_map=None` inside a CPU context, safetensors only and
local files only, with no quantizer/automatic placement configuration admitted.

HF normally copies registered custom model code with source file permissions.
The new kernel disables that copying only on its new model/config instances and
instead writes exactly the two current Lumi runtime modules exclusively at 0600,
verifying their bytes afterward. The original `auto_map` and self-contained
package are preserved. Other code files or changed runtime-module bytes fail.
Output directories are 0700 and the temporary private umask is restored on failure.
Old classes, source modules, v2 behavior and prior evidence are unchanged.

## Real metadata arithmetic, no model allocation

The five original metadata inputs total **15,599 bytes**. Their hashes, the
complete handoff declarations and the original tokenizer inventory semantics
pass. The exact 616-byte source config also passes the new closed source schema
without importing ML. The model is still our step-76,294 / 80B-token base.

The analytical inventory has **4,002,376,704 unique parameters**, **363 state
entries**, 36 persistent FP32 rotary buffers / 1,152 buffer elements, and the
deliberately wider 4,096 query dimension over a 3,072 residual width.

| BF16 planning quantity | Bytes |
| --- | ---: |
| Complete checkpoint declarations | 48,839,117,190 |
| Projected live tensor payload cap, including staging/scratch | 26,044,312,576 |
| Conservative host-RAM planning minimum | 68,719,476,736 (64 GiB) |
| Backend output cap | 8,408,459,776 |
| Output plus tokenizer/control write allowance | 8,415,205,181 |
| Staged input/output disk planning minimum | 57,258,970,624 |
| Two full checkpoint inventory audits | 97,678,234,380 |

These are **computed planning values, not measured RSS/disk requirements or an
execution approval**. The audit figure excludes DCP restore and HF/output reload
reads; those still need a real runner limit. Host/library/allocator headroom is
not OS-enforced by this kernel. The original 1 MiB backend overhead allowance
contains the real config/header/code projection of 958,728 bytes. Tokenizer copy
and final control files have a separate allowance. FP32 alternative planning is
80 GiB host RAM; no alternate dtype or host was selected for execution.

Saved plan: `configs/lumi-4b-sft-checkpoint-import-plan-v0.1.json`, 70,554 bytes,
file SHA-256 `3138f88ddb871c0a24134a5aae84cb28128581b8d943ca599f24553bd3c3cbd8`.
Its internal SHA-256 is
`6b193d89febd605717ce7c5ef18f9d3355fc63f221c5b21b986e15e759c0231a`.
Original floating-point JSON representation is retained so the saved internal
binding matches the Python planner; structural verification recalculates it.

## Verification

There are **98 new tests**: 25 planner, 34 stream, 38 CPU kernel and one combined
tiny integration test. Including regressions, **282 distinct tests pass**:

- 226 stdlib/source tests on Python 3.11.15, 3.13.7 and 3.14.6, respectively
  0.285s, 0.279s and 0.288s; no failures or skips.
- 56 genuine tiny CPU tests on Python 3.13.7, including 17 unchanged v2 CPU
  regressions; 0.442s test time / 1.691s supervised wall time under a 60s timeout.
  These use torch 2.10.0, transformers 5.2.0, safetensors 0.7.0,
  tokenizers 0.22.2 and numpy 2.2.6; no packages were installed. No failures/skips.
- Exact Ruff E,F,I,B,UP / py311 / line length 100 passes for all seven new code/
  test files. The integration test binds a tiny synthetic metadata plan, hashes
  its entire checkpoint, performs actual CPU DCP-to-HF conversion and compares
  the second complete unchanged audit. Synthetic trainer placeholders are hashed
  but never deserialized. It is not a real-base receipt.

Review/test findings were fixed before the final pass, including late deadline
success, malformed-row key-copy bounds, unsafe config kwargs, output expansion,
private custom-code copying and CPU reload compatibility. Native Linux/GPU
qualification has not been performed. Known pinned DCP deprecation/platform
warnings are not reported as failures or silently turned into acceptance.

Aggregate evidence: `evidence/lumi-4b-sft-import-components-v0.1.json`, 8,008 bytes,
SHA-256 `27edab822b2a4b629644b68faf2b1f649273c7fb94bae8736abbbd3d9bafaba9`.
Structural verification:
`evidence/lumi-4b-sft-import-components-verification-v0.1.json`.
It independently checks ten new/runtime file bindings, five frozen predecessor
bindings, the saved plan's internal digest, resource/count arithmetic and false
execution claims. It does not claim to rerun tests or verify real payloads.

## Next existing dependency, not another data-policy gate

The **full real-import runner remains unwired**. Next integrate these implemented
components with a separately bound dedicated host/runtime, immutable read-only
inputs, actual read/RAM/runtime/write enforcement, exclusive no-retry attempt
marker, tokenizer copy, private import receipt and frozen worker checksum consumer.
Then qualify that runner on native Linux and perform the explicitly bounded real
checkpoint restore when the execution target and limits are approved.

GPU-compatible worker/profile, actual data relocation and CUDA/smoke requirements
remain downstream as described in v0.70. No GPU is needed for the remaining
runner code work. Gate 5, real SFT and production eligibility remain false.

All new files are local/unpublished. HEAD remains
`ecc545fc423650bf1089311915290523c10b3172`; tracked/staged diffs are unchanged.
